LOCAL ACCEPTANCE HARNESS NOTE

The first disposable PostgreSQL 17 matrix invocation used a randomly assigned
host port. It stopped in the historical M2 command harness with a connection
timeout before reaching any S9 or S10 test. The container was removed by the
pre-registered cleanup trap. No product, repository or server state changed.
The R2.1 acceptance is repeated with an explicit isolated port and a host-side
readiness probe before the S9/S10-only migration and full-CLI path are run.

The first targeted S9/S10 full-path invocation reached the new bounded runtime
envelope but returned nonzero. Its raw output was intentionally not emitted.
The disposable database was removed by the cleanup trap and no external state
changed. The acceptance now reports only safe code, stage and exit class on a
failure so the remaining synthetic-fixture difference can be resolved without
exposing the credential or driver message.

The safe envelope classified that targeted attempt as database-connect at the
rotation database stage. The shortened fixture had applied migrations without
first creating the historical NOLOGIN runtime role that the full acceptance
runner creates before its grant migrations. Consequently the intended
connection-time SET ROLE failed. This was a fixture-construction error, not a
product failure. The disposable database was again removed without external
state change.

After creating the synthetic runtime role before applying the migrations, the
PostgreSQL 17 full-path acceptance completed green. It used the reviewed module
entrypoint, absolute contract and copy paths, fixed working directory, bounded
environment, runtime role and synthetic database. The successful rotation was
followed by two controlled unreachable-database runs. Both returned the same
database-connect class, rotation database stage, exit class 22 and deterministic
fingerprint; credential content was absent from stdout and stderr.

